  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 141 PM CDT Sun Sep 20 2026\n\nPatchy drizzle remains possible late this afternoon into the evening \nahead of the incoming rainfall. The rain will move into the area \nthrough the afternoon into tonight. Low pressure to the southwest \nwill swing saturation and lift aloft into the area this afternoon \nwith a dry slot working north towards the Missouri River. This seems \nto suggest that the better potential for a more steady rainfall will \nbe closer to I-90 as the dendritic layer dries out south of I-90 \nduring the evening, but remains saturated near and north of I-90. \nModel soundings do hint at some weak instability near the Missouri \nRiver which may help overcome the lack of saturation in the \ndendritic layer during the evening and overnight hours, but the \nrainfall would be more scattered in nature. If this weak instability \ndoes not come to fruition, drizzle will be more likely towards the \nMissouri River. Given how weak any expected instability will be, \nthunderstorms are not expected.\n\nAs for amounts through tomorrow, model agreement is fairly high in a \nswath of a half an inch to an inch and a half, focused near I-90 in \nsoutheast SD, then east into northern parts of northwest IA. These \nrainfall amounts are not expected to cause any flash flooding or \nrapid rises along rivers.\n\nMonday will see lingering showers across the area, with a more \nfocused area over northwest IA as the upper level low slowly moves \nout of the area. Amounts will be lower through the day on Monday, \nbut parts of northwest IA could see a quarter inch of additional \nrainfall. Otherwise Monday will be cool and cloudy with highs from \nthe upper 50s to mid 60s.\n\nMonday night into Tuesday morning could see some fog with light \nsoutheast flow and a fairly moist boundary layer.\n\nTuesday and Wednesday should both be dry and cool with highs in the \n60s both days. Winds will increase on Wednesday with gusts around 30 \nmph possible near and west of the James RIver.\n\nWednesday night into Thursday will be the next better chance for \nshowers and thunderstorms. At this time severe weather and heavy \nrain look unlikely.\n\nTemperatures will rebound back to around normal Friday into next \nweekend as westerly flow aloft develops. Periodic chances for \nshowers and thunderstorms are expected during this time.",
